dict(sec=9, img='B_afFast', cap=CH,
stem="A 76-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of 1 week of increasing breathlessness. His pulse is 142/min and irregular, and blood pressure is 128/76 mm Hg. His electrocardiogram is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely rhythm?",
choices=["Atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response","Atrial flutter with variable conduction","Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Sinus tachycardia with frequent atrial premature beats","Wandering atrial pacemaker"], key=0,
exp="An <b>irregularly irregular</b> narrow-complex rhythm (no two R–R intervals alike when marched out), with <b>no organized P waves</b> and an undulating baseline, is <b>atrial fibrillation</b>, here with a rapid ventricular response. The lecturer's reassurance: irregular with clean narrow complexes is almost always atrial fibrillation. The QRS is usually narrow unless there is a bundle branch block, an accessory pathway or rate-related aberrancy.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Atrial fibrillation: irregularly irregular R–R intervals, no P waves and a variable ventricular rate.",
wrong={1:"Flutter with variable conduction can be irregular, but it shows organized sawtooth waves at about 300/min.",
2:"Multifocal atrial tachycardia shows discrete P waves of three or more shapes before the QRS complexes.",
3:"Sinus tachycardia with premature beats shows sinus P waves before most complexes and a pattern of occasional early beats.",
4:"A wandering atrial pacemaker has discrete P waves of varying shape at a rate under 100."},
eli5="The atria are quivering instead of beating, so there are no P waves, and the ventricles get signals at random. The pulse is completely irregular: atrial fibrillation."),

dict(sec=9, img='B_afCase', cap=CH,
stem="A 78-year-old woman comes to the office because she can no longer climb the stairs to her bedroom. She has had 3 weeks of fatigue. Her pulse is irregularly irregular at 104/min. Her electrocardiogram is shown. She denies any sensation of a racing or pounding heartbeat.{FIG}Which of the following best describes the usual presentation of atrial fibrillation?",
choices=["Chest pain is the most common presenting symptom","Fatigue and dyspnea outnumber palpitations","Most patients notice palpitations at the onset","Most patients present with a stroke","Syncope is the usual first symptom"], key=1,
exp="The lecture's point: atrial fibrillation is <b>not mainly palpitations</b>. <b>Fatigue and shortness of breath</b> — heart-failure-type complaints such as \"I can't climb a flight of stairs\" — are the most common symptoms; only about <b>50–60%</b> of patients feel palpitations at all. A vignette of new exertional breathlessness in an older patient with an irregular pulse describes atrial fibrillation, not just deconditioning. Losing the atrial kick and shortening diastolic filling time reduce cardiac output.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> The most common symptoms of atrial fibrillation are fatigue and shortness of breath; only about half of patients notice palpitations.",
wrong={0:"Chest pain can occur with a rapid rate in coronary disease, but it is not the most common presentation.",
2:"Only about half of patients feel palpitations, so many present without them.",
3:"Stroke is the feared complication and sometimes the first sign, but it is not how most patients present.",
4:"Syncope is uncommon in atrial fibrillation unless there is a conversion pause or severe structural disease."},
eli5="Most people with atrial fibrillation don't feel their heart racing. They just feel tired and short of breath because the heart pumps less efficiently."),

dict(sec=9, img=None,
stem="Two patients are seen in an atrial fibrillation clinic. Patient 1 had an episode that stopped on its own after 5 days. Patient 2 had atrial fibrillation continuously for 3 weeks that required electrical cardioversion; she now has occasional brief episodes.{FIG}Which of the following best classifies the two patients?",
choices=["Both have paroxysmal atrial fibrillation","Both have persistent atrial fibrillation","Patient 1 paroxysmal; patient 2 permanent","Patient 1 paroxysmal; patient 2 long-standing persistent","Patient 1 paroxysmal; patient 2 persistent","Patient 1 persistent; patient 2 long-standing persistent"], key=4,
exp="Definitions to know for the wards:<br>• <b>Paroxysmal</b>: intermittent, terminates within <b>7 days</b> (patient 1).<br>• <b>Persistent</b>: continuous for <b>more than 7 days</b> and requiring an intervention — drug, cardioversion or procedure (patient 2). It <b>stays \"persistent\" even if later treatment converts it to a paroxysmal pattern</b>.<br>• <b>Long-standing persistent</b>: continuous for more than 12 months.<br>• <b>Permanent</b>: patient and clinician jointly decide to stop trying to restore sinus rhythm — a treatment decision, not a disease state.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Paroxysmal atrial fibrillation (AF) ends within 7 days; persistent AF lasts over 7 days and needs intervention (and stays classified as persistent); permanent AF is a joint decision to stop pursuing sinus rhythm.",
wrong={0:"Patient 2's episode lasted 3 weeks and needed cardioversion, which makes it persistent even if episodes are now brief.",
1:"Patient 1's episode ended on its own within 7 days, which is paroxysmal.",
2:"Permanent atrial fibrillation is a decision to stop restoring sinus rhythm; patient 2 was cardioverted.",
3:"Long-standing persistent requires more than 12 months of continuous atrial fibrillation; patient 2's lasted 3 weeks.",
5:"Patient 1 terminated within 7 days, and long-standing persistent requires more than 12 months of continuous atrial fibrillation."},
eli5="If it stops by itself within a week, it's paroxysmal. If it lasts longer and needs a shock or drug to stop, it's persistent, and that label sticks."),

dict(sec=9, img=None,
stem="A 72-year-old woman with hypertension and type 2 diabetes mellitus is found to have atrial fibrillation at a routine visit. She has no history of heart failure, stroke or vascular disease.{FIG}Which of the following is her CHA₂DS₂-VASc score?",
choices=["2","3","4","5","6"], key=2,
exp="CHA₂DS₂-VASc: <b>C</b>ongestive heart failure 1 · <b>H</b>ypertension 1 · <b>A</b>ge ≥75 2 · <b>D</b>iabetes 1 · <b>S</b>troke/transient ischemic attack (TIA)/thromboembolism 2 · <b>V</b>ascular disease (prior myocardial infarction (MI), peripheral arterial disease, aortic plaque) 1 · <b>A</b>ge 65–74 1 · <b>S</b>ex category female 1.<br>Her score: age 65–74 (1) + hypertension (1) + diabetes (1) + female (1) = <b>4</b> (3 by the newer CHA₂DS₂-VA, which drops the sex point). A score of <b>2 or more usually means anticoagulation</b>, now usually with a direct oral anticoagulant rather than warfarin.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Compute CHA₂DS₂-VASc (age 65–74 = 1, ≥75 = 2, prior stroke = 2, others 1 each); a score of 2 or more usually warrants anticoagulation.",
wrong={0:"2 counts only the hypertension and diabetes, omitting age 65–74 and female sex.",
1:"3 omits the female-sex point (the newer CHA₂DS₂-VA score would give 3).",
3:"5 counts age as 2 points, which applies only at 75 or older.",
4:"6 adds points for stroke, which she has not had."},
eli5="Add a point each for her blood pressure, her diabetes, her age range (65–74) and being female: that's 4. Two or more means she should be on a blood thinner."),

