exp="The lecture's first acute question is <b>how long it has been going on</b>, because duration decides whether cardioversion is safe. Atrial fibrillation lasting <b>more than 48 hours or of unknown duration</b> may have produced a <b>left atrial (appendage) thrombus</b>, and restoring organized contraction can dislodge it. Before elective cardioversion, either <b>anticoagulate for about 3 weeks</b> or perform <b>transesophageal echocardiography</b> to exclude thrombus (then cardiovert under anticoagulation), and continue anticoagulation for at least 4 weeks afterward (board add-on, AMBOSS algorithm).<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Atrial fibrillation of more than 48 hours or unknown duration needs about 3 weeks of anticoagulation, or transesophageal echocardiography to exclude atrial thrombus, before cardioversion.",
